            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of James Madison</strong><br>\nJames Madison was born on March 16, 1751, in Port Conway, Virginia, to a wealthy family of planters. He was the eldest of twelve children. From a young age, Madison was a voracious reader, and his education was given high importance. He attended the College of New Jersey, now known as Princeton University, where he studied a range of subjects and completed his course in two years. This early education laid the foundation for his future role in American politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Beginnings</strong><br>\nMadison's political career began in the early 1770s when he served on the local Committee of Safety, a pro-revolution group. He was a delegate to the Virginia Constitutional Convention in 1776 and became a state legislator. His work during this period included shaping Virginia's constitution and championing religious freedom, reflecting his belief in a government that protects individual rights.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The \"Father of the Constitution\"</strong><br>\nJames Madison's most distinguished contribution was at the Constitutional Convention in 1787. As a delegate, Madison proposed the Virginia Plan, which outlined a strong federal government with three branches. He took detailed notes during the convention, which have become a primary source for understanding the U.S. Constitution's formation. His influence was so significant that he is often called the \"Father of the Constitution.\"</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
