            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Federalist Papers and the Bill of Rights</strong><br>\nMadison, along with Alexander Hamilton and John Jay, wrote the Federalist Papers, a series of essays advocating for the Constitution's ratification. After the Constitution was adopted, Madison realized the necessity of a Bill of Rights. He drafted the first ten amendments to the Constitution, ensuring the protection of individual liberties such as freedom of speech, assembly, and religion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Secretary of State and the Louisiana Purchase</strong><br>\nBefore becoming president, Madison served as Secretary of State under President Thomas Jefferson. During his tenure, he supervised the Louisiana Purchase, which doubled the size of the United States. He also dealt with the trade restrictions imposed by Britain and France during the Napoleonic Wars, which affected American merchants and set the stage for the War of 1812.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Presidency and the War of 1812</strong><br>\nMadison was elected president in 1808 and re-elected in 1812. During his presidency, the U.S. declared war on Britain, marking the beginning of the War of 1812. The war was a response to British restrictions on U.S. trade and America's desire to expand its territory. The war concluded in 1815 with the Treaty of Ghent, which largely restored relations between the U.S. and Britain.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
