          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Post-War of 1812 America</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Madison oversaw the \"Era of Good Feelings,\" a time when political parties were less divisive. He supported the creation of a second national bank and the implementation of a protective tariff to support American industry. These measures helped stabilize the post-war economy and foster a sense of national unity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Retirement and Later Years</strong><br>\nJames Madison retired from the presidency in 1817 and returned to his Virginia plantation, Montpelier. He remained active in public affairs, helping to establish the University of Virginia alongside Thomas Jefferson. In his later years, Madison became a prominent advocate for the historical importance of the Constitution and the principles of republican government.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nJames Madison died on June 28, 1836, at Montpelier. His legacy includes his role as a key architect of the U.S. Constitution, the Bill of Rights, and his leadership during a formative period of the United States. As a president, Madison faced immense challenges, yet his contributions shaped the nation in its early years and have continued to influence American politics and law.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
